#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177031 :tutorial/content "In general, the logistic growth boundary value problem for the function $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y), \\qquad y(0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0,$ and $0 < y < a.$\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00478583a9bc76980b898db4f846f323511026c1c6ac7b79e3f047575a015305.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- In practical situations, we usually set $t > 0.$ If we relax this constraint, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to-\\infty}{y(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\n \nThis behavior can be confirmed using an analysis similar to what we saw earlier.\n\n- The parameter $a$ is the **carrying capacity,** and is the limiting value of $y(t)$ for large $t.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = a.\n$$\n\n \nIf $y(t)$ represents how some population changes with time, the carrying capacity gives the maximum possible population.\n\n- We have assumed that $0 < y < a$ for all $t.$ Other cases are possible, though we do not consider those here." :tutorial/id #uuid "3f070df6-1d9d-5050-b509-d48d330c4709" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5714 :tutorial/title "Some Key Features of a Logistic Growth Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177033 :tutorial/content "For some differential equations, it's possible to sketch the solution curves *without* calculating the general solution. Instead, we gather information about the solutions using the differential equation itself.\n\nAs an example, consider the following logistic growth equation: \n\n$$\ny' = 5y(10-y)\n$$\n\nIn practical applications, we're usually interested in the domain $t > 0,$ so we will stick to this constraint for the moment.\n\nThe equation is autonomous, so let's start by finding the equilibrium solutions. Setting up the equation $y'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n5y(10-y) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, there are two equilibrium solutions, $y=0,$ and $y=10.$ Let's create a diagram and plot these two solutions.\n\n![](images/98/98d52420673e65eedf5679e57b9c562f8a8eeabde24d0d2ed4fff827a6447fc2.png)\n\nWe will now sketch a solution curve that lies between these two equilibrium solutions. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- Since $0 < y < 10$ for all $t,$ we have that $5y(10-y)$ is positive for all $t.$\n\n- Therefore, $y'(t)$ is positive for all $t.$\n\n- Hence, $y(t)$ is increasing for all $t.$\n\nUsing this information, we can start to sketch a solution curve.\n\n![](images/ce/ce6f493f4c2830acf42e189ace04027f8452ec3df92d24b9d223f92848832996.png)\n\nNext, we note the following regarding the end-behavior of our solution:\n\n- The expression $5y(10-y)\\to 0$ as $y\\to10.$\n\n- Therefore, $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to 10.$\n\nIn other words, the curve flattens as $y$ approaches $10.$ Adding this behavior to our diagram, we get a complete sketch of the solution curve.\n\n![](images/f3/f329f3cd49d61c3a1e2838397521cdd6d3bbefc7dadd09d1dd25b00767a0cd37.png)\n\nGathering information about the solutions to a differential equation without solving it is called **qualitative analysis.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "05ebc248-7295-5381-964a-30c7e3b638e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177034 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to the following logistic growth equation:\n\n$$\ny' = 5y(10-y), \\qquad y(0) = y_0.\n$$\n\nThe logistic growth curve for this problem is sketched below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a225fd04f81bd65e1bad3ff14097563d112a16e3847aac26cc86591533ea5d0.png)\n\nThe curve contains an inflection point, that is, a point where $y(t)$ is growing at its fastest possible rate. What information can we deduce about this point? \n\nTo find the inflection point, we solve $y''(t) = 0.$ First, we differentiate the differential equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =(5y)^{′}\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (10-y)^{′} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (10-y)\\cdot {y}^{′}+(5y)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {y}^{′} \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (5\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (50-5y-5y) \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (50-10y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to solve $y''(t) = 0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny'\\cdot\\left(50-10y\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe know that $y'$ is increasing for all $t,$ so $y'\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n50-10y = 0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad y = 5.\n$$\n\nLet's mark this on our diagram.\n\n![](images/75/75d0363483e1bda70decb171466fe9e506eb0da80d0e78ed893bb61551ae9d44.png)\n\nThe fact that the inflection point occurs when $y$ is precisely half the carrying capacity is *not* a coincidence. In fact, this is always the case. The general situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7754f42adbb938c1d07ab2636a4c24f1cd0bc8536c974af2497e997589efa115.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9032224d-2537-52e4-a688-8e341433ca18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5719 :tutorial/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase"}]}
